The design synchronizes `sclk`, `mosi`, and `cs_n` into the `clk` domain with two-flop synchronizers. A third register holds the previous `sclk` value, so a rising edge is `sclk_s && !sclk_prev`.

Mosi is sampled at the same moment the rising edge is detected, using the synchronized mosi, which is aligned with the synchronized `sclk`. Both pass through the same number of flops, so the data seen at the detected edge is the value at the `sclk` rising edge. Since the system clock is at least 8 times faster than `sclk`, mosi changes only after the falling edge of `sclk`, so this is safe.

While synchronized `cs_n` is high, the bit count is cleared and the shift register is left alone. The previous-`sclk` register keeps tracking `sclk` in all cases, so a rising edge is not falsely detected when `cs_n` falls.

On each rising edge with `cs_n` low, the bit is shifted into a shift register. If the count is 7, the completed byte `{shift[6:0], mosi_s}` is loaded into `data`, `valid` is pulsed for one cycle, and the count returns to 0. `valid` defaults to 0 every cycle and is set only in that case.

Reset values for the synchronizer flops: `cs_n` resets to 1 (idle) and `sclk` to 0, so no spurious edges occur after reset.

