- `rd_data` is loaded from `mem[rd_ptr]` on an accepted read.

```systemverilog
module sync_fifo (
    input  logic       clk,      // clock
    input  logic       rst_n,    // active-low synchronous reset
    input  logic       wr_en,    // write request
    input  logic [7:0] wr_data,  // data to write
    input  logic       rd_en,    // read request
    output logic [7:0] rd_data,  // data of the most recent accepted read (registered)
    output logic       full,     // 1 when 4 items are stored
    output logic       empty     // 1 when 0 items are stored
);

    logic [7:0] mem [0:3];
    logic [1:0] wr_ptr, rd_ptr;
    logic [2:0] n;
    logic       do_wr, do_rd;
    logic [2:0] n_next;

    always_comb begin
        do_wr = wr_en && !full;
        do_rd = rd_en && !empty;
        n_next = n;
        if (do_wr && !do_rd)
            n_next = n + 3'd1;
        else if (!do_wr && do_rd)
            n_next = n - 3'd1;
    end

    always_ff @(posedge clk) begin
        if (!rst_n) begin
            wr_ptr  <= 2'd0;
            rd_ptr  <= 2'd0;
            n       <= 3'd0;
            rd_data <= 8'd0;
            empty   <= 1'b1;
            full    <= 1'b0;
        end else begin
            if (do_wr) begin
                mem[wr_ptr] <= wr_data;
                wr_ptr      <= wr_ptr + 2'd1;
            end
            if (do_rd) begin
                rd_data <= mem[rd_ptr];
                rd_ptr  <= rd_ptr + 2'd1;
            end
            n     <= n_next;
            empty <= (n_next == 3'd0);
            full  <= (n_next == 3'd4);
        end
    end

endmodule
```

The FIFO is a 4-entry memory with 2-bit read and write pointers and a 3-bit count `n`. `full` and `empty` are registered outputs, and the accept conditions use those registered flags, as the operation table specifies.

Accept logic:
- `do_wr = wr_en && !full`
- `do_rd = rd_en && !empty`

The table's cases fall out of these two conditions:
- With `wr_en=1`, `rd_en=1`, `full=0`, `empty=0`, both are accepted and `n` is unchanged.
- With `full=1` and `empty=0`, only the read is accepted, so `n` decreases by one.
- With `empty=1` and `full=0`, only the write is accepted, so `n` increases by one.

A simultaneous read and write when `n` is between 1 and 3 reads the oldest item, which is already stored, so there is no bypass hazard. At `n=0` the read is ignored, so no bypass is needed there either.

On a read, `rd_data` is loaded from `mem[rd_ptr]` and `rd_ptr` increments. On a write, `mem[wr_ptr]` is loaded and `wr_ptr` increments. The next count `n'` is computed combinationally, then `full'` is set when `n'==4` and `empty'` when `n'==0`.

The memory is left without a reset, which is fine because it is only read after being written.

