The design is a 4-entry circular buffer with 2-bit read and write pointers and a 3-bit item count. `full` and `empty` are registered and updated from the next count.

Accept conditions follow the spec: a write is accepted when `wr_en` is 1 and `full` is 0, and a read is accepted when `rd_en` is 1 and `empty` is 0. These are evaluated independently, so a write while full is ignored even if a read is accepted in the same cycle (diagram 3).

A simultaneous read and write while empty accepts only the write, since the read is ignored. When both are accepted, the read takes the oldest item from memory. The write targets the slot at the write pointer, which differs from the read slot unless the FIFO is empty. An accepted read never occurs when empty, so there is no conflict.

I checked this against diagram 2. At cycle 4, with one item (P) stored, writing Q and reading P in the same cycle gives `rd_data` = P, and Q is stored.

